00:06:35.085 --> 00:06:38.623
(upbeat jazz)

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:43.040
(mysterious music)

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:47.200
Madison gets home to find her husband, Derek,&nbsp;
watching a UFC match on TV, which depicts a

00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:50.720
fighter punching his opponent in the back of the&nbsp;
head. This is a foreshadowing of the incident that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.720 --> 00:06:54.480
would soon occur, where Derek pushes his wife into&nbsp;
a wall and she cracks open the back of her head,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:58.480
allowing Gabriel to surface. There are a couple&nbsp;
instances in the movie of butterfly motifs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.480 --> 00:07:01.840
like the wallpaper at the Simeon facility&nbsp;
and Sydney’s outfit when she visits Madison&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:06.160
in the hospital. A butterfly takes flight only after&nbsp;
coming out of its cocoon. It kind of reminds me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:09.440
of the image of Gabriel poking his head out&nbsp;
of the back of Madison’s skull. It’s hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:12.960
to say if that was intentional. The analogy&nbsp;
isn’t perfect, because Gabriel could never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:17.200
“take flight” and leave Madison’s body, because&nbsp;
he’s just a parasite, he needs her to survive.

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:20.640
But there are some more concrete clues&nbsp;
about who and what Gabriel really is.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:23.280
When he strikes Derek, if you’re paying&nbsp;
close attention, you’ll notice that his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:26.720
hand is upside-down from what you’d expect.&nbsp;
Try it right now. Take your right hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:31.040
and do a nice overhead smack with your fingers&nbsp;
pointing down. Unless you’re extremely flexible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:35.520
this will feel very unnatural. That’s because this&nbsp;
is actually the left hand going behind the back.

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:39.681
The police do pick up on this, but have a&nbsp;
different theory about how it was achieved.

00:07:39.681 --> 00:07:43.440
– [Det. Moss] No fingerprints, but all the&nbsp;
hand patterns are upside-down.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:45.280
Like our perp was hanging from the ceiling.

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:49.280
– They also note that there was no forced entry&nbsp;
into the house because Gabriel was already inside.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:53.360
Derek’s head is twisted around 180° degrees,&nbsp;
causing his face to essentially look backwards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:56.480
just like Gabriel. He’s fixing his victims&nbsp;
so that they look like him. We also see him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.480 --> 00:07:59.680
do this at the jail, where one of the victim’s&nbsp;
arms is pulled back in the opposite direction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:03.200
of the joint, and at the police station where&nbsp;
an officer’s limbs are bent back the wrong way.

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:04.720
Madison tries to cope by saying

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:07.333
– It’s all in my head… It’s all in my head.&nbsp;

00:08:07.333 --> 00:08:11.360
– She’s correct, but it’s not her imagination,&nbsp;
it’s literally in the back of her head.

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:17.851
– [Gabriel] You know who I am. Even if&nbsp;
they say I’m only in your head.

00:08:17.851 --> 00:08:21.120
– When she was young, the surgeons cut out as&nbsp;
much of the cancer as possible, only leaving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:24.880
what was necessary for Madison to survive. The&nbsp;
rest of Gabriel was buried under her skull.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.880 --> 00:08:29.120
The way the tour guide describes Old Seattle&nbsp;
seems symbolic of what happened to Gabriel.

00:08:29.120 --> 00:08:33.920
– This is a tour of old Seattle. Buried underneath here like a&nbsp;
time capsule of a bygone era.

00:08:33.920 --> 00:08:37.600
– Of course, after her skull is cracked open,&nbsp;
Gabriel’s face can push its way back to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:41.280
surface, which may be the inspiration for&nbsp;
the wording of this line by Detective Moss.

00:08:41.280 --> 00:08:45.360
– If I read about one more sick&nbsp;
kid, my brain’s gonna push out my eyeballs.

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:48.240
– [Zac] I mentioned that Gabriel is able to&nbsp;
essentially hijack Madison’s brain and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:51.760
make her see visions of the crimes he commits&nbsp;
when he’s in control. In her hallucinations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:53.920
Gabriel often emerges from&nbsp;
the backside of her head,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.920 --> 00:08:57.840
first during the death scene of Dr. Fields,&nbsp;
then again when Gabriel comes for Dr. Gregory.

00:08:57.840 --> 00:09:00.640
After each hallucination, she awakens&nbsp;
with fresh stains on her pillow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:02.640
because Gabriel had pushed his way out.

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:05.360
When Madison uses the bathroom at the&nbsp;
police station, we can see the back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.360 --> 00:09:08.320
of her head in the mirror. Is it just&nbsp;
me, or is there something mysterious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.320 --> 00:09:13.840
about the dark part in her hair right&nbsp;
here? Kif, I’m asking you a question.

00:09:13.840 --> 00:09:17.520
After escaping from Detective Shaw, it becomes&nbsp;
more clear that he’s running backwards, and he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:21.360
continually inverts himself when he's fleeing down&nbsp;
the fire escape. The scene reminds me a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:25.760
John Doe’s escape in Se7en, which makes me think&nbsp;
that James Wan was probably also inspired a lot by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.760 --> 00:09:30.240
fellow director David Fincher. The biggest Fincher&nbsp;
connection though, is in Malignant’s soundtrack.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:33.840
There will be major spoilers for Fight Club, so&nbsp;
if you haven’t seen it, skip to the end of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.840 --> 00:09:37.600
chapter. Fight Club has a somewhat similar&nbsp;
twist ending. The main character has a split&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.600 --> 00:09:41.760
personality, and at the end, he purges him by&nbsp;
killing off the 2nd personality. In Malignant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:45.680
the voice in Madison’s head is more of a literal&nbsp;
person, but she defeats him by locking him away in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:49.840
her mind. Fight Club famously ends with the song&nbsp;
“Where Is My Mind?” by The Pixies. Like if you look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.840 --> 00:09:53.840
up the song on YouTube, the top comments are all&nbsp;
about Fight Club, the two are that intertwined.

00:09:53.840 --> 00:09:56.480
The song that we hear over and over&nbsp;
again during the transitions in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.480 --> 00:09:59.624
Malignant is clearly inspired by "Where Is My Mind?"

00:10:00.848 --> 00:10:09.360
(cinematic music)

00:10:10.560 --> 00:10:12.400
It seems to be a little clue from James Wan that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.400 --> 00:10:15.200
Malignant shares more than one&nbsp;
thing in common with Fight Club.

00:10:15.200 --> 00:10:18.560
But as I mentioned, the biggest source&nbsp;
of inspiration seems to come from giallo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.560 --> 00:10:22.240
an Italian genre of mystery / thriller /&nbsp;
horror stories, primarily in the 60s and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.240 --> 00:10:25.920
70s. The way Gabriel fashions himself is&nbsp;
very much like a typical giallo villain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:29.440
but I’ll save that for when I cover Gabriel on&nbsp;
Horror History. But there are other examples,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.440 --> 00:10:33.040
though, like when the TV turns on, the first&nbsp;
thing we hear sounds like an Italian film.

00:10:33.040 --> 00:10:35.440
(italian voices)

00:10:35.440 --> 00:10:39.280
Dr. Weaver keeps her records in “Bellagio&nbsp;
Italia” binders. When Gabriel comes for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.280 --> 00:10:43.120
Dr. Fields at his apartment, the place is&nbsp;
illuminated in red neon light. Colorful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.120 --> 00:10:46.320
neon lighting is a hallmark of giallo&nbsp;
films, and they often depict a clash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:50.240
of red and blues. Along with Gabriel’s &nbsp;
silhouette, this scene is clearly an homage.

00:10:50.240 --> 00:10:53.040
As a side note, the red light is&nbsp;
being emitted from this sign outside,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.040 --> 00:10:57.200
which says Silvercup. Silvercup is a production&nbsp;
facility in New York City. I’m guessing parts of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.200 --> 00:11:01.200
Malignant were filmed there, but as for the sign,&nbsp;
it’s not quite the same as the real life sign,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:03.840
so I’m guessing they created that&nbsp;
in a sound studio. In the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:07.760
Silvercup is an apartment complex, not a&nbsp;
production studio. Interestingly, Silvercup&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.760 --> 00:11:11.200
was also a working title for Malignant.&nbsp;
It’s not a very big part of the story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.200 --> 00:11:14.960
so I’m guessing it was just used early&nbsp;
on to hide what the movie might be about.

00:11:14.960 --> 00:11:17.280
But the scene across from the&nbsp;
Silvercup sign also shares a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.280 --> 00:11:21.000
huge similarity with another horror&nbsp;
film from James Wan’s filmography.

00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:22.920
(impact)

00:11:25.974 --> 00:11:29.131
(dark music)

00:11:29.760 --> 00:11:33.200
The way that Gabriel appears from the open&nbsp;
window in Dr. Fields’s apartment is very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.200 --> 00:11:36.880
reminiscent of another James Wan villain&nbsp;
appearance, this one in a motel during his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.880 --> 00:11:41.600
2007 film Dead Silence. The antagonist, Mary&nbsp;
Shaw, appears in front of the flowing curtain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:45.840
illuminated only by the buzzing neon sign across&nbsp;
the way. In fact, when Madison sees the vision of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.840 --> 00:11:50.000
Dr. Fields’s fate, the red light flickers in a&nbsp;
very similar manner to the Mary Shaw encounter.

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:54.160
That brings me to my next Wan connection.&nbsp;
The name “Shaw” comes up a lot in his work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.160 --> 00:11:59.200
whether it’s Dead Silence’s Mary Shaw, Malignant’s&nbsp;
Detective Kokoa Shaw, or even Furious 7’s Deckard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.200 --> 00:12:03.440
Shaw and Owen Shaw… although Deckard Shaw did&nbsp;
debut in the previous film, Fast &amp; Furious 6,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.440 --> 00:12:06.800
which James Wan was not involved with,&nbsp;
and technically Deckard Shaw did too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.800 --> 00:12:10.320
but he only appeared in the end credits,&nbsp;
so he’s mostly a Furious 7 character.

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:14.480
Speaking of non-horror movies directed by Wan,&nbsp;
his previous effort before Malignant was called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.480 --> 00:12:18.000
Aquaman. Yeah, they literally made a movie out&nbsp;
of Aquaman.

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:21.206
– [Narrator] Fame led to book deals, movie appearences and hit records!

00:12:21.206 --> 00:12:26.480
– [Zac] I guess Detective Shaw must be a fan. When he’s playing the video on his laptop, the&nbsp;
Aquaman logo appears in the bottom corner of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.480 --> 00:12:30.400
his screen -- it’s not there before. His&nbsp;
first name is also Kekoa. The actor who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.400 --> 00:12:35.120
plays the young version of Aquaman is named Kekoa&nbsp;
Kekumano. So there’s definitely a reference there.

00:12:35.120 --> 00:12:39.440
There might also be a Saw connection with&nbsp;
him. He’s part of an asian / black detective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.440 --> 00:12:44.080
duo. I can’t help but wonder if this is a nod to&nbsp;
Detectives Sing and Tapp from the original Saw.

00:12:44.080 --> 00:12:47.600
Normally I don’t talk about actor cameos, but&nbsp;
I do find it a little bit interesting that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.600 --> 00:12:51.680
both the young and old versions of Madison are&nbsp;
played by actresses from the Annabelle movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.680 --> 00:12:54.880
which are produced by James Wan as&nbsp;
part of his Conjuring franchise.

00:12:54.880 --> 00:12:57.120
Before I talk about the significance&nbsp;
behind the character names,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.120 --> 00:13:00.880
there are a few more points that I’d like&nbsp;
to mention. So… might as well do them here.

00:13:00.880 --> 00:13:03.520
The first tape we see from Dr.&nbsp;
Weaver has a timecode at the top,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.520 --> 00:13:07.280
and once it gets to 11:59, it just&nbsp;
resets back to 11:00 instead of moving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.280 --> 00:13:10.880
onto twelve. Probably just a glitch that no&nbsp;
one noticed, I just found it kind of weird.

00:13:10.880 --> 00:13:14.560
Then, also in the opening sequence when Gabriel&nbsp;
is rampaging, there’s a child’s picture on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.560 --> 00:13:18.480
wall depicting thunderclouds and lightning. That&nbsp;
might just be a normal day for kids in Seattle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.480 --> 00:13:21.680
but it could be connected to Gabriel's&nbsp;
ability to manipulate electricity.

00:13:21.680 --> 00:13:25.600
Then during the 2nd “break-in” that isn’t really&nbsp;
a break-in, the camera circles around Madison,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.600 --> 00:13:29.200
and we see both sides of her head. This is a&nbsp;
great setup for the big reveal of Gabriel, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.200 --> 00:13:33.280
it makes his presence even more shocking. Blaming&nbsp;
everything on an evil twin is one of the oldest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.280 --> 00:13:37.600
excuses in the book, but I guess this is one&nbsp;
of those rare times where it is also actually the truth.

00:13:37.600 --> 00:13:42.080
Sydney drives a Toyota Prius, which is a hybrid&nbsp;
vehicle. Madison later drives this car as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.080 --> 00:13:45.600
Madison could be considered a hybrid&nbsp;
of Gabriel and Emily May. Honestly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.600 --> 00:13:48.800
it’s probably just product placement. I&nbsp;also 
thought that maybe the fact that Madison&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.800 --> 00:13:52.080
and Gabriel have the same phone was a&nbsp;
clue at one point, but then I realized,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.080 --> 00:13:54.800
literally everyone in the movie&nbsp;
has this phone for some reason.

00:13:54.800 --> 00:13:58.400
OK, so I already talked about the religious&nbsp;
implications behind the name Gabriel. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.400 --> 00:14:01.680
while this character is clearly linked to the&nbsp;
supernatural, the other names in the story are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.680 --> 00:14:06.240
named after real things found in nature. Sydney’s&nbsp;
last name is “Lake”, the female detective is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.240 --> 00:14:10.880
“Moss”, one of the doctors is “Fields”, and even&nbsp;
“Shaw” is an English-Scottish name meaning “one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.880 --> 00:14:15.200
who dwells by the woods”. Sydney’s first name may&nbsp;
also be an example, the name Sydney means “wide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.200 --> 00:14:19.600
meadow”; and Serena’s first name obviously&nbsp;
means serene, clear and tranquil, all words&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.600 --> 00:14:23.520
usually used to describe nature. Also, there’s&nbsp;
a woman in jail who’s nickname is Scorpion.

00:14:23.520 --> 00:14:27.280
That just leaves our main character, Madison&nbsp;
Mitchell, whose birth name was Emily May.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.280 --> 00:14:31.360
Madison and May are both connected. Madison&nbsp;
traditionally means “son of Matthew” while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.360 --> 00:14:35.360
May references the Goddess Maia, or the&nbsp;
“Family of Matthew”. If for some reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.360 --> 00:14:39.280
someone watching the movie knew that, it could be&nbsp;
a clue that these characters are one in the same.

00:14:39.280 --> 00:14:43.040
If you’re a fan of James Wan’s horror movies,&nbsp;
like Saw, Dead Silence, Insidious and The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.040 --> 00:14:46.960
Conjuring you’re in luck, because I have a whole&nbsp;
playlist with my analysys on his other works,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.960 --> 00:14:51.120
which you can find on the left. Remember to&nbsp;
subscribe to CZsWorld for new horrors every week,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.120 --> 00:14:53.600
ring the deathbell and select&nbsp;
all notifications, and…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.240 --> 00:15:00.160
why do we always end it like this… I’ll see&nbsp;
you in the next one, assuming we both survive.

